The robustness tradeoff
Rerandomization has an appealing operational story and preserves multiple acceptable assignments. Its weaknesses are equally instructive:

a stringent threshold lowers the acceptance probability and concentrates the assignment distribution;
Mahalanobis balance is only as relevant as the chosen covariates and feature representation; in high dimensions, covariance regularization also changes the metric;
for a fixed acceptance probability, increasing dimension weakens the attainable improvement in each direction and makes covariance estimation or regularization more consequential;
unrecorded changes to the acceptance rule invalidate the stated design;
analysis that pretends complete randomization was used mischaracterizes uncertainty.

The threshold therefore mediates balance against robustness. Making \(a\) smaller improves balance along the chosen covariate directions, but it leaves fewer possible assignments and makes the design more dependent on whether those directions capture the outcome structure that matters. Here robustness includes both protection against a misspecified balance criterion and enough randomness to support credible, reproducible inference. Perfect observed balance is not the only goal of a randomized experiment.

Weak probabilities enter the variance
The same representation shows what happens before probability reaches zero. Subtracting the design expectation of the \(i\)th summand in Equation 3 gives
\[
(Z_i-\pi_i)
\left\{
\frac{r_i(1)}{\pi_i}
+\frac{r_i(0)}{1-\pi_i}
\right\}.
\tag{4}\]
Under independent Bernoulli assignment, contributions from different units are independent and \(\operatorname{Var}_Z(Z_i)=\pi_i(1-\pi_i)\). Therefore
\[
\operatorname{Var}_Z\{\widehat\tau(g)\}
=\frac{1}{N^2}\sum_{i=1}^N
\pi_i(1-\pi_i)
\left\{
\frac{r_i(1)}{\pi_i}
+\frac{r_i(0)}{1-\pi_i}
\right\}^2.
\tag{5}\]
The formula makes two mechanisms visible.

A small \(\pi_i\) magnifies the treated residual \(r_i(1)\); a value near one magnifies the control residual \(r_i(0)\).
Better fixed predictions can reduce the residuals, but cannot change which assignments the design permits.

The formula is exact for independent Bernoulli assignment. Under complete randomization, rerandomization, or another dependent design, cross-unit covariances also enter. The support conclusion is unchanged: weighting can correct unequal positive probabilities, but it cannot manufacture a treatment state with probability zero.

Adaptive trimming changes the inferential problem
Suppose an estimated score \(\widehat k\) and threshold \(\widehat\gamma\) define
\[
\widehat{\mathcal A}
=\{x:\widehat k(x)\le\widehat\gamma\}.
\]
For sampled covariates \(X_1,\ldots,X_n\), the realized target is
\[
\tau_{\widehat{\mathcal A}}^{\mathrm{sample}}
=\frac{
\sum_{i=1}^n
\mathbf 1\{X_i\in\widehat{\mathcal A}\}
\{m_1(X_i)-m_0(X_i)\}
}{
\sum_{i=1}^n
\mathbf 1\{X_i\in\widehat{\mathcal A}\}
}.
\]
This differs from the effect for a fixed population rule \(\overline{\mathcal A}\) defined by population quantities. The two are close only under conditions on boundary estimation and the probability mass near that boundary. Khan and Ugander develop cross-fitted, doubly robust inference for the realized sample-adaptive target, including simultaneous intervals over prespecified trimming procedures (2025). That result does not automatically become inference for a fixed limiting target. Data-adaptive target selection is part of the inferential question, not preprocessing to forget.

Other ways to construct the missing comparison
Low rank connects two familiar approaches. A synthetic control predicts a treated unit’s counterfactual from other units’ contemporaneous outcomes. For a single treated unit \(i_\star\), choose donor weights using pretreatment data, then predict \(\widehat Y_{i_\star t}(0)=\sum_{j\in\mathrm{co}}w_jY_{jt}^{\mathrm{obs}}\) in post-treatment periods. The canonical construction uses nonnegative weights summing to one (Abadie, Diamond, and Hainmueller 2010). Under a factor model, a successful comparison reproduces the treated unit’s relevant loadings. The restrictions also limit extrapolation: the toy example’s coefficients \((2,1)\) are not convex weights, so its algebraic completion is not a canonical synthetic control.
A horizontal prediction instead learns, among controls, how later outcomes relate to earlier outcomes and applies that relation to the treated units’ earlier outcomes. These are different uses of the panel: synthetic control learns combinations across units that continue over time, while horizontal prediction learns combinations across times that continue across units. Matrix completion estimates a shared representation that links both directions.

Weighted differences rather than an entire completed matrix
Sometimes the target is one average effect, not every missing entry. Synthetic difference-in-differences combines unit and time weighting with the removal of additive differences (Arkhangelsky et al. 2021). Consider a common adoption date, control units \(\mathrm{co}\), treated units \(\mathrm{tr}\), pretreatment periods \(\mathrm{pre}\), and post-treatment periods \(\mathrm{post}\). Define the treated mean in period \(t\) as \(\overline Y_{\mathrm{tr},t}\), and choose control weights \(w_j\) and pretreatment time weights \(v_t\), each nonnegative and summing to one.
For fixed such weights, the weighted DID contrast is \[
\begin{aligned}
\widehat\tau(w,v)&=
\frac{1}{T_{\mathrm{post}}}
\sum_{t\in\mathrm{post}}
\left(\overline Y_{\mathrm{tr},t}
-\sum_{j\in\mathrm{co}}w_jY_{jt}^{\mathrm{obs}}\right)\\
&\quad-\sum_{t\in\mathrm{pre}}v_t
\left(\overline Y_{\mathrm{tr},t}
-\sum_{j\in\mathrm{co}}w_jY_{jt}^{\mathrm{obs}}\right),
\end{aligned}
\tag{21}\] where \(T_{\mathrm{post}}\) is the number of post-treatment periods. It compares the treated-control difference after treatment with a weighted version of that same difference before treatment.
To see the benefit, hold weights fixed and ignore noise. Under the factor model, the remaining untreated-mean discrepancy is \[
\underbrace{\left(
\overline{\mathbf u}_{\mathrm{tr}}
-\sum_{j\in\mathrm{co}}w_j\mathbf u_j\right)^\top}
_{\text{difference in factor loadings}}
\quad
\underbrace{\left(
\overline{\mathbf f}_{\mathrm{post}}
-\sum_{t\in\mathrm{pre}}v_t\mathbf f_t\right)}
_{\text{difference in factor averages}}.
\tag{22}\] Here bars denote arithmetic averages over the indicated group or periods. Unit intercepts cancel when taking the weighted time difference; common time effects cancel in the unit comparison. The interaction discrepancy is a product. Matching loadings well or matching the relevant time-factor average well can reduce it; neither requires explicitly interpreting each latent factor.
The actual method learns weights from observed outcomes. Unit weights match treated and weighted-control pretreatment trajectories up to a constant, with regularization to discourage concentration. Time weights make a weighted pretreatment average resemble post-treatment averages among controls, again allowing a constant. Neither fit uses treated post-treatment outcomes as an untreated target. The learned weights then enter a weighted two-way fixed-effects regression, equivalently Equation 21.
The product in Equation 22 explains a mechanism, not an unconditional guarantee for data-fitted weights. Good pretreatment fit must generalize into the missing block; noisy adaptive weights and residual dependence require additional analysis. Nor are these weights assignment probabilities like those in the seeding experiment. They construct comparisons from outcomes under structural assumptions.
